-Dear Hiring Manager,
+I am writing to apply for the Vice President of Information Technology role at MBX Biosciences. Having spent the last seven and a half years building and running enterprise IT for a pharmaceutical company through the exact arc your posting describes - from pre-commercial to first U.S. product launch to roughly $1B in annual commercial operations - I recognize the shape of this job. I also reported into a CFO for much of that period, so the reporting line and the expectation of speaking Finance's language on technology investment are both familiar territory.
 
-I'm writing about the Vice President of Information Technology role at MBX Biosciences. For the past seven-plus years I've been doing a version of this job: I joined SK Life Science in September 2018 when the US entity had two IT staff, two contractors and roughly $3M of annual pre-commercial IT spend, and I left in January 2026 as Head of IT, the CIO-equivalent, with an $8M budget and an enterprise supporting around $1B in annual commercial sales. The arc your posting describes - pre-commercial to commercialization, with research, clinical, commercial and corporate all needing to be served properly - is the arc I've already walked.
+At SK Life Science I moved from Enterprise Architect to Vice President, Head of Applications, to Head of IT, and each step involved standing up capability that didn't yet exist rather than simply maintaining what was there. I built the enterprise application architecture that carried our first U.S. launch to market within fourteen months, including SAP S/4HANA, Veeva CRM and R&D, drug serialization, and an MDM foundation that improved reporting accuracy and operational efficiency by 35%. On the commercial side, I owned the systems - CRM, commercial analytics, payer and market-access segmentation - that sales, market access, and patient services actually relied on day to day, which is the same commercial-enablement remit your posting calls out.
 
-The commercial readiness piece is where I'd expect to be most immediately useful. I owned the enterprise application ecosystem that carried the company to its first U.S. product launch inside 14 months: Veeva CRM, SAP S/4HANA, DSCSA serialization, patient services and specialty pharmacy dispense feeds, market access and gross-to-net inputs for Finance, HCP/HCO mastering, and the commercial analytics layer on Snowflake and Power BI. I worked directly with the Chief Commercial Officer and VP of Commercial Operations on incentive compensation reporting, territory alignment and demand forecasting, so I understand where field, market access and patient support systems tend to break down before launch rather than after. Roughly 400-500 users were trained through the SAP and Veeva go-lives, with over 90% active use in the first 90 days.
+The AI and cybersecurity pieces of this role are ones I've lived rather than studied. I led AI-enabled initiatives inside a GxP-regulated environment - automating first-line security triage, building a next-best-action model for our Medical Science Liaisons, and flagging expense anomalies - while keeping validated systems like SAP and Veeva as the system of record and requiring human review before any AI output drove action. That governance discipline matters more in a regulated life sciences company than the technology itself, and it's where I'd expect to spend real time at MBX as you formalize your own AI strategy. On the security and compliance side, I ran a cybersecurity program aligned to SOC 2 and NIST frameworks alongside SOX ITGC and Part 11 validation, cut operating expense by 30% through vendor renegotiation and cloud rightsizing, and never missed an SLA doing it - across roughly 650 users spanning U.S. and Korea sites plus a remote workforce, along with Day-1 technology integration for two M&A transactions.
 
